# naming the harness, three of which are the harness itself.
RECORD_BLOCKS = 132

# The one marker the tripwire counts, and the two phrasings records use INSTEAD of
# naming the harness. Both are read by the same walk, so the blind spot below is a
# subtraction between two figures measured by one loop on one tree - never a
# constant, and never two snapshots taken at different times.
HARNESS = "break-once.sh"
UNNAMED = ("Made red", "Red under")

# ── THE WALK COVERS WHAT A `lives_in` CAN BE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE RULE ───────
#
# It used to be `*.rs` only, with a note here saying whoever widened it must pass
# `markers_for`. This is that widening, and the markers are passed per file.
#
# **The rule is not a list of interesting directories - it is that the walk must cover
# every file a registered record can live in, or the coverage figure lies.** Registering
# a shell row under an `*.rs` walk would report it as a record the tripwire cannot see:
# a false `unseen`, manufactured by the act of registering. So `scripts/*.sh` and
# `.githooks/*` are in because they hold records, and `.mise/tasks/*` is OUT because no
# row lives there - an exclusion that is a consequence rather than an entry on a list.
#
# **Passing `markers_for` is what makes the widening real, and whether forgetting it is
# SILENT depends on when.** A shell file read with the Rust markers contributes ZERO
# blocks. So:
#
#   * widening the glob and forgetting the markers, in one edit, moves NOTHING —
#     `RECORD_BLOCKS` stays where it was, the tripwire stays green, and the walk reports
#     a population it cannot see. Silent, and the defect the `#` arm exists for one
#     level up.
#   * removing `markers_for` AFTER the constant has been set from a widened run is
#     LOUD. Watched, by making that exact cut: **exit 1**, `FAIL population: 127 comment
#     blocks mention break-once.sh, but RECORD_BLOCKS says 132.`
#
# So the constant converts the omission from silent to loud, and only once somebody has
# set it from a correct run. That ordering is the whole value of pinning it.
all_blocks: list[str] = []
for path in sorted(
    list((ROOT / "keylink").rglob("*.rs"))
    + list((ROOT / "keylink-persist").rglob("*.rs"))
    + list((ROOT / "scripts").glob("*.sh"))
    + [p for p in (ROOT / ".githooks").iterdir() if p.is_file()]
):
    text = path.read_text()
    all_blocks += comment_blocks(text, markers_for(str(path.relative_to(ROOT)), text))
harness_blocks = [b for b in all_blocks if HARNESS in b]
marked_blocks = [b for b in all_blocks if HARNESS in b or any(p in b for p in UNNAMED)]
blocks = len(harness_blocks)
blind = len(marked_blocks) - blocks

# ── coverage: an INTERSECTION, because the two populations are different units ──
#
# The first version of the line below printed `RECORD_BLOCKS - len(ANCHORS)`, which
# **subtracts a row count from a block count**. It read as coverage and it was wrong
# in the FLATTERING direction - 77 unchecked against a true figure of 89 on the tree
# that carried it. Two reasons the subtraction cannot work:
#
#   * several rows can live in ONE block, so rows do not map onto blocks 1:1; and
#   * a row's record need not be a harness block at all. `rows_unseen` is the rows that
#     are not, and the summary PRINTS it rather than this comment naming it: their
#     records say "Made red by .." without naming `break-once.sh`, the population this
#     tripwire is declared blind to above. Those rows cannot be "of the counted
#     population" in any sense. The figure sat here as "Eight of them are not" beside a
#     population of "102", and exactly ONE of those two rotted. The population is
#     whatever `RECORD_BLOCKS` now says, which the tripwire keeps true and which is no
#     longer 102. The ROW figure never rotted: `rows_unseen` measures **8 at
#     `e06b7bd`, the commit that wrote that sentence, and 8 at `96cf374`**, and the
#     computed line below prints today's. So "Eight" was accurate then and is accurate
#     now, and it moved onto that line for the reason every figure here does - not
#     because it was wrong, but because nothing would say so once it became wrong.
#     Calling both stale would invert this branch's own finding: the records it
#     corrects claimed MORE redness than they had, and a correction claiming more
#     staleness than there was errs in the direction that flatters the correction.
#
# `882bd35` on this branch is the same defect one level up - **a gate cannot claim a
# denominator it cannot compute.** So the numbers below are an intersection measured
# both directions, and nothing is subtracted across units.
registered = {flat(literal) for _, _, _, literal in ANCHORS}
harness_spans = [spans(block) for block in harness_blocks]
blocks_carrying = sum(1 for spans in harness_spans if spans & registered)
quotable_anywhere = set().union(*harness_spans) if harness_spans else set()
rows_unseen = [rid for rid, _, _, lit in ANCHORS if flat(lit) not in quotable_anywhere]

if blocks != RECORD_BLOCKS:
    bad += 1
    print(f"FAIL population: {blocks} comment blocks mention break-once.sh, but "
          f"RECORD_BLOCKS says {RECORD_BLOCKS}.", file=sys.stderr)
    print("       A record was added or removed. Decide whether it needs a row",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       above - most will not, and that is fine - then set the constant",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"       to {blocks}, the number counted from THIS tree just now.",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       This exists so the decision is forced, not skipped.",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print(file=sys.stderr)
    # Two sentences that exist because of how this failure is actually met.
    #
    # RE-MEASURE, NEVER CARRY. The constant is a property of a tree, so a value
    # brought across a rebase is stale the moment a sibling branch adds or removes a
    # record. Measured three times on three trees inside one day: 102, then 110, then
    # 111. Twice the arithmetic happened to agree, which is worse than it failing —
    # an agreeing subtraction reads as a derivation. `parked-drain`'s figures are the
    # counter-example that settles it: carrying blocks went 13 → 15 while the
    # population went 102 → 103, so the unchecked count FELL, 89 → 88. A subtraction
    # whose inputs both move is how a stale figure keeps looking current.
    print("       RE-MEASURE, never carry: this constant describes ONE tree, so a",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       value brought across a rebase is stale as soon as a sibling",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       branch adds or removes a record. Do not compute it either - an",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       agreeing subtraction reads as a derivation and is a coincidence.",
          file=sys.stderr)
